## How to Build a Men's Emerald Cut Ring

Most of the settings in this guide require choosing a center stone separately. The process is straightforward but the order matters.

Start with the setting. Pick your frame shape — bypass, hexagon, channel, signet, or nugget — and confirm the band width. Then find an emerald cut stone whose face-up width matches the band.

A 7mm band fits a 1ct emerald cut. A 9mm band needs a 2ct emerald cut to fill proportionally.

For the stone: choose lab grown over natural for men's rings at 2ct and above. The [lab grown emerald cut search](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/emerald-cut?type=lab-grown&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) gives you 2ct VS1 stones at $1,500–$2,500 instead of the $12,000–$20,000 a natural 2ct VS1 would cost.

Use VS1 minimum clarity — the open table on an emerald cut shows inclusions that would be invisible in a round brilliant. For color, G–H is the sweet spot: colorless enough to look clean in white gold, warm enough to work with yellow gold without looking grey.

